/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - ARINC 429 carries 100 kbit/s one way, and an airliner would need hundreds of such pairs.
   - Office Ethernet is best effort: a busy switch may delay or drop a frame, and nobody can say how long a frame will take.
   - A virtual link (VL) is a one-way logical path from one source end system to one or more destinations, named by a 16-bit VL identifier carried in the frame's destination address.
   - BVL is the reserved bandwidth in bit/s; Lmax is in bytes (×8 gives bits); BAG is in seconds.
   - The end system also limits jitter, the extra delay a frame meets while waiting behind frames of other VLs at the output, to 500 µs.
   - Redundancy: each end system has two ports, one on network A and one on network B, with separate switches, cables and power.
   - A fibre link has a light source (an LED or a laser diode), the fibre and a photodetector (a PIN or avalanche photodiode).
   - n₁ is the core index and n₂ the cladding index; θc is measured from the normal to the boundary; NA = sin of the acceptance half-angle in air.
   - Multimode fibre (core 50 or 62.5 µm, usually at 850 nm) is cheap and easy to connect, and suits the short runs inside an aircraft.
   - A link works if the received power stays above the receiver sensitivity with a safety margin.
*/
